 the neuroblastoma whole  exome sequencing specifically it does  tell you that that was done at the Broad  Institute you if you click on that link  you can go specifically into a single  run and see all of the information  surrounding that what sample was used  what submission it was in anything you  would need to know metadata wise and  just to get through the identifiers all  of these runs and experiments and other  metadata are stored in sra numbers that  could be difficult to interpret so here  it just provided you with a list of what  these accession numbers stand for so if  it gets confusing you know what that  means however as I mentioned you cannot  get Bam's without a special download  client you do need the approval through  the DB gap to get to the target data  this does need to be renewed every year  you can download and install a spare  client locally on your own computers and  if you navigate the DB gap website and  download appropriate files you currently  to a degree need to look up IDs but this  is being changed right now NCBI to try  to make the process a bit easier and our  target DCC person  are happy to provide detailed  instructions for how to access this  quickly to go over it I showed you the  login page earlier but if you want to go  through the dbgap interface so that you  can learn more about how the database  for genotypes and phenotypes works if  you come to their main page you will be  able to go to apply for control data  access and it will take you to where you  need to go previously to login and to  navigate here to my requests so my  requests is how we put in the request  for approval but when you're actually  going to download the data what you will  go to is my projects because approved  users already have this loaded up into  that tab it will look similar to this  and you'll be able to just click on the  target data and what you'll find is  something similar to this this is ncbi's  new run selector where you can actually  sort by the information that you'd like  to obtain so if you specifically want  this is an example again from  neuroblastoma which is PHS number four  six seven but you'll note that each of  our sub-projects has a different  three-digit number at the end and the  targets parent study is to 18 so if you  want to sort by specifically whole exome  sequencing that was done at the road you  can do that if you want to sort by males  you can do that sort by tumor samples or  normal samples and that way you can get  the data set that you're looking for  prior to actually downloading I can tell  you if you do not do that in advance  downloading this material if you try to  get everything that's within target it's  close to 3,000 data files and it will  take a lot of storage and about three  months to download and this you can see  a bit of a tree view that you can also  use to get at the data you want if you  do want everything is certainly can  um and and then you would just rather  than using the run selector to select a  subset of cases you can just go through  the tree view and and check everything  again you must have a spare up  downloaded and you can check on the data  manifest if you would like for what is  available again this is going to be  thousands of rows so it may not be the  most efficient but if you have a script  for a different way that you'd like to  parse you could use it on this Dana  manifest so in addition to target there  are two other current programs on  knowing and the office of cancer  genomics including as I mentioned before  another large-scale initiative similar  to TCGA but studying different cancers  as the cancer genome characterization  initiative as well as a more  translational program that pulls  together the data generated from these  large-scale projects and that is known  as the cancer target discovery and  development or CT t squared network and  just very briefly CGC I is a  multi-project initiative and there are  some completed projects that were  looking at medulloblastoma and naj  non-hodgkin lymphoma the publications  from those that i have listed initial  publications here there are other  publications that can be found at the  website listed on this page for more  information but all of that data is also  available you apply for it in the same  way that you apply for target data or  for that matter for TCGA data even  though it goes through cghub you still  do use the DBA process for ongoing  programs in CGC I right now are the HIV  positive to learn molecular  characterization program which is  studying lung cancer diffuse large  b-cell lymphoma and hpv-related cancers  in hiv-positive patients as well as the  burkitt lymphoma genome sequencing  project which is looking at birgit  lymphomas many of them from overseas in  Africa the cases are being characterized  follows with mrna sea of the tumor RNA  or DNA sequencing a whole genome or  exome of match to more normal DNA and  again verification of candidate  mutations is going to occur same as it  will with target and as i mentioned cg b  squared is meant to bring all of this  large-scale genomic data together and  rapidly translate it for the patient  benefit or precision medicine this is  surrounding high-throughput  identification and characterization of  potential targets for cancer therapy be  it jeans or pathways as well as  identification of small molecules that  modulate validated cancer targets found  through either the CPG square network  themselves or through these large-scale  genomics initiatives there's an emphasis  on interactions of centers so the CPP  squared network was piloted as five  center network that did publish their  results and the data was so successful  with the team science and with the  approach that the network has been  expanded to 13 centers and those centers  will focus on bioinformatics genome-wide  loss-of-function screening targeted  gain-of-function gene validations Mouse  based in vivo screening protein-protein  interactions and small molecule high put  three high-throughput screening share  the cg d squared network will share  validated candidates with the cancer  community allowing investigators to  contribute to the understanding of the  mechanisms of cancer and potentially  accelerate development of clinically  useful markers and therapeutics for  evidence-based treatments as is the goal  with all of our projects discussed here  today and as I mentioned the ARRA  funding allowed for the expansion of the  CTD squared project into what you have  here and each Center has different  disease expertise you can go to the URL  listed on the previous page to find out  more information and use this data so  with that I would like to acknowledge  our NCI staff in the ocg program office  dr. daniela Gerhard is our director and  I of course manage most of the pediatric  programs dr. Megan Stein is another  program manager on our office who is  managing the CGC I efforts cancer  therapy evaluation program dr. Malcolm  Smith is co-directing the target  initiative and certainly was key and its  inception our Oct communications team  doctors Shannon berman and Jessica  mosaic and of course our OC g data  coordinating Center team who handles all  of our data dr. Tanya Davidson and  senior bioinformatics engineers patty  gasso on and Leander hermida I thank you  all for your participation and I hope  that you will visit our site and use our  data and help us to come up with ways to  treat our patients and if you would like  to obtain continuing education credits  please follow the link on this page  thank you
 

